-- title:
--   PM ✱1: literals of a valuation (for completeness)
-- statement:
--   **DRAFT** — part of an in-progress public formalization of *Principia Mathematica*; it may still change, and every change is dated in the project's [change log](https://github.com/willmcginnis/principia-mathematica-lean/blob/main/docs/EXPORT_LOG.md).
--
--   For a truth value $b$ and a formula $\varphi$, $\varphi^b$ is $\varphi$ if $b$ is true and ${\sim}\varphi$ otherwise. For a valuation $v$ and a variable $x$, the *literal* $x^v$ is $x^{v(x)}$; for a list of variables $[x_1,\dots,x_n]$ the literals are the list $[x_n^v,\dots,x_1^v]$.
--
--   These are the hypotheses of Kalmár's lemma in the completeness proof of PM's ✱1 for truth tables (the card `PM.meta_complete`).
--
--   Source: [PM.Complete](https://github.com/willmcginnis/principia-mathematica-lean/blob/2e43991fec4221198853c15ba637b6c8430cec4b/PM/Complete.lean).
-- source:
--   Whitehead and Russell, Principia Mathematica, Vol. I (1910), ✱1–✱5; formalization: https://github.com/willmcginnis/principia-mathematica-lean/blob/2e43991fec4221198853c15ba637b6c8430cec4b/PM/Complete.lean

